  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1257 PM CDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nSoutherly flow out ahead of a weak trough across north central South \nDakota early this afternoon, with 60 dewpoints out ahead and \n30s/40s to near 50 dewpoints coming in from the west. Winds on the \nwest side of the trough are only at 10 to 15kts so despite rh% \ndropping to around 10%, no need for Red Flag.  \n\nRegarding smoke, with return flow today the current airmass \ncontains little to no low level smoke. Likewise, little to no \nsmoke is depicted in the post frontal environment in the HRRR \neither. The Pacific Northwest will remain the source region of \nconcern over the next several days, which is typically not \nfavorable for low level smoke due to mixing as it crosses the \nNorthern Rockies. Aloft, this is a very favorable source region \nespecially in this northwesterly to westerly zonal flow pattern \nwhich persists through most of the forecast period (through the \nmiddle of next week). \n\nSo for today, looking at some weak convection already but still \nconsidered elevated. CAMS continue to support an isolated cell or \ntwo in the afternoon across central/northeast South Dakota, but NAM \nBUFKIT profiles suggest its still overall capped in this area, with \nthe trough well back to the west of where they are generating \nconvection. Just added a 20% probability of a storm and will leave it \nat that.\n\nA cold front gets a little better organized tonight as it shifts \nthough the area. This does two things 1) better forcing for isolated \nconvection and 2) increased low level flow. Aside from low POPs \nthere isn't much left to say about convection. If we have any fires \nthis evening then the winds shifting and increasing will be a concern \nfor fire management.  NBM manages wind grids well, as 1/2km winds \nincrease to 30kts on the 'cold' advection side of the system and \nincreases surface winds to 25 to 30kts. NBM also hints at a re-\ninvigoration of the downslope winds this evening/overnight across \nthe northeast. \n\nA northwest flow regime continues more or less continuously \nthrough at least the middle of next week. Late Sunday/Monday is\nthe next/best opportunity for moisture in the NBM, derived from\na northwest flow shortwave that traverses the Northern Rockies,\nand combines with a plume of monsoon moisture traveling around \nthe 4-Corners high. This flow regime also lends towards near- \naverage temperatures and more temperate humidity.",
